steps.text:
We can test with lint and snapshots. Let's design the HTML with inline styles and test.

Important: the 3D canvas should render at panel rectangles but viewport scaling. renderer.setSize(1920,1080,false) with canvas CSS 1920x1080. devicePixelRatio may be 1 in render; setPixelRatio(1) for determinism. Snapshots at 1920. Use antialias.

Potential screenshot issue: renderer canvas may not be captured if hf-seek handler renders after screenshot? The runtime likely calls hf-seek and awaits font/media; synchronous WebGL draw should be okay.

Need to check how hf-seek is dispatched: e.detail.time. Also root timeline seek triggers GSAP; our renderAt called on hf-seek. At load, renderAt(0). But when the scene is embedded, maybe hf-seek fires. We are root, so yes.

For build, avoid async; build scene at script end then register timeline.

GSAP timeline must be registered after scene builds. Fine.

Need to update HUD in renderAt too (timecode, force, stage highlight, progress, panel label active). Position projection labels? skip.

Dough text label on board: canvas texture text "A. DOUGH" engraved. It's small; nice in hero but top view may read. Use canvas 512x128, fill text, color dark brown. Ball tray label "B. STRESS BALL". Alternatively HTML labels projected—more legible. Let's do 3D placards: a thin plate in front of each station with text texture. In hero, visible; in front view, facing camera? If plate lies flat on table, front view can't read. If upright, top view can't read. Maybe both: small engraved text on table surface is readable from top view, and 3D object shapes are self-evident. Skip 3D text; HUD stage rail handles naming. But panel labels could show "A" marker near object via projected HTML tags. Let's consider: each panel has two small station tags "A" and "B" positioned via projecting world coords to screen in renderAt, translated to panel coordinates. Then the tags move with camera only if camera moves (fixed), so static positions. Could compute once. Adds clarity: small circles at the objects with labels "DOUGH" and "STRESS BALL". But 3 tags × 3 panels = 6 tags, busy. Maybe only hero panel gets tags. And bottom rail highlights active. Actually stage rail chips with live indicator enough.

Maybe add a small caption in hero panel bottom-left that changes: "A — DOUGH / 6.2 N" then "B — STRESS BALL / 9.8 N". This is clean and informative. It's HTML inside hero area, updates text in renderAt. Good.

End: 8.8s closing overlay in hero: "ONE HAND. TWO MATERIALS. THREE VIEWS." + small "CAPTURED IN SYNC". Use Inter 300 letter spacing. Avoid ad cliché? It's a study; a closing line is fine. User asked only action; maybe no end card needed. But a designed video benefits. I'll include a short end line fading in at 9.0 and holding to 10.

Audio plan: generate a low synth pad + servo + squish foley. Let's think about whether audio adds risk. The CLI render includes audio if present. Media-use skill required for any audio? The task doesn't require music; the skill says a film over ~15s that tells something gets music. Ours is 10s. Sound would be a bonus. But "画面全部用代码生成" maybe they expect visuals only; adding code-generated sound is consistent.

I can generate a WAV with Node: 
- ambient pad: 55Hz + 110 + 164.8 sine with slow amplitude, filtered noise.
- servo whir during hand movement: sawtooth at 120-180Hz with vibrato and noise, amplitude synced to movement segments.
- pinch/contact: soft squish (filtered noise burst with pitch drop) for dough; rubbery squeak (sine glide) for ball.
- releases: small servo tick.
- end: warm chord swell.
This is doable in a script. 30-60 lines. Do after visuals. Need ffmpeg to encode wav if needed; `<audio src="assets/audio/score.wav">` supports wav. 10s stereo 44.1k wav = 1.7MB fine.

Audio element needs id and data-volume etc. Place at root with data-start="0" data-duration="10" id="score" data-volume="1".

Now let's code the composition. This will be a large file. I'll structure it carefully.

Let me set up assets/vendor:
- three.min.js r160.
- gsap 3.14.2 min.

Then write index.html.

Three scene construction:

```js
const DUR = 10;
const canvas = document.getElementById('gl');
const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({canvas, antialias:true, preserveDrawingBuffer:true, alpha:false});
renderer.setPixelRatio(1);
renderer.setSize(1920,1080,false);
renderer.outputColorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;
renderer.toneMapping = THREE.ACESFilmicToneMapping;
renderer.toneMappingExposure = 1.05;
renderer.shadowMap.enabled = true;
renderer.shadowMap.type = THREE.PCFSoftShadowMap;
const scene = new THREE.Scene();
scene.background = new THREE.Color(0x0b0a09);
scene.fog = new THREE.FogExp2(0x0b0a09, 0.045);
```

Camera creation per panel with fov.

Environment map from canvas:
```js
function envTexture(){
  const c=document.createElement('canvas'); c.width=1024;c.height=512;
  const g=c.getContext('2d');
  // dark base gradient
  ...
  // strips: white/warm rectangles with gradients at y=140, 200 etc
  const tex = new THREE.CanvasTexture(c);
  tex.mapping = THREE.EquirectangularReflectionMapping;
  tex.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;
  return tex;
}
scene.environment = envTexture();
```
Use PMREM? scene.environment can be an equirect texture directly; three auto-converts? Since r131, assigning equirect texture to scene.environment works and renderer converts internally? Actually WebGLRenderer supports equirect env maps directly for PBR (it uses envMap with mapping). It does work. For better blur could PMREM. Let's use PMREMGenerator:
```js
const pmrem = new THREE.PMREMGenerator(renderer);
const envRT = pmrem.fromEquirectangular(tex);
scene.environment = envRT.texture;
```
Needs renderer initialized. Fine.

